TI Temperature changes on the Tibetan Plateau during the past 600 years
   inferred from ice cores and tree rings
SO GLOBAL AND PLANETARY CHANGE
LA English
DT Article
DE Puruogangri ice cap; climate change; ice cores; tree rings; Tibetan
   Plateau
ID CENTRAL HENGDUAN MOUNTAINS; QAIDAM BASIN; PRECIPITATION VARIATION;
   ENVIRONMENTAL-CHANGES; SUMMER MONSOON; CLIMATE-CHANGE; POLLEN RECORD;
   SOURCE REGION; RECONSTRUCTION; VARIABILITY
AB The climatological signal of delta O-18 variations preserved in ice cores recovered from Puruogangri ice field in the central Tibetan Plateau (TP) was calibrated with regional meteorological data for the past 50 years. For the period AD 1860-2000, 5-yearly averaged ice core delta O-18 and a summer temperature reconstruction derived from pollen data from the same ice core were compared. The statistical results provide compelling evidence that Puruogangri ice core delta O-18 variations represent summer temperature changes for the central TP, and hence regional temperature history during the past 600 years was revealed. A comparison of Puruogangri ice core delta O-18 with several other temperature reconstructions shows that broad-scale climate anomalies since the Little Ice Age occurred synchronously across the eastern and southern TP. and the Himalayas. Common cold periods were identified in the 15th century, 1625-1645 AD, 1660-1700 AD, 1725-1775 AD, 1795-1830 AD. 1850-1870 AD, 1890-1920 AD, 1940-1950 AD, and 1975-1985 AD. The period 1725-1775 AD was one of the most prolonged cool periods during the past 400 years and corresponded to maximum Little Ice Age glacier advance of monsoonal temperate glaciers of the TP. (C) 2009 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
