TI Air pollution recovery still falls short of pre-industrial conditions:
   sulfur stable isotope analysis of tree rings from two giant trees
SO BIOGEOCHEMISTRY
LA English
DT Article
DE Air pollution; Dendrochemistry; Sulfur isotope ratio; Tree ring analysis
ID ACIDIC DEPOSITION; EMISSIONS; FOREST; WOOD; RECONSTRUCTION; WATER;
   ACIDIFICATION; SIGNATURES; INVENTORY; ERUPTIONS
AB Reconstructing long-term records of air pollution and comparing present-day air quality with that of the pre-Industrial Revolution era are crucial for predicting the trajectory of recovery from sulfur-based air pollution. The annual rings of trees contain sulfur compounds that have accumulated over time, and the sulfur stable isotope ratio (delta 34S) reflects the past delta 34S values in the atmosphere. Although delta 34S in tree rings has been analyzed in the past, there are no delta 34S data in tree rings from before the Industrial Revolution (1760), when humans began to emit large amounts of sulfur compounds to the atmosphere. Therefore, we analyzed long-term variations in delta 34S in the annual rings of two giant Japanese cedars (Cryptomeria japonica) from the Okute-Shinmei Shrine and Ise Jingu in central Japan, spanning over 350 years. The fluctuation trend of delta 34S values was statistically divided into periods before and after industrialization. Before industrialization, delta 34S values were stable and high, averaging + 6.0 +/- 0.6 parts per thousand in Okute and + 10.5 +/- 0.9 parts per thousand in Ise. The values closely match previously reported adjusted delta 34S values from Greenland ice cores. After industrialization, values declined sharply owing to fossil fuel use. This decline stabilized around the 1990s, and values increased slightly. Despite these reductions in sulfur emissions, delta 34S values remain significantly lower than pre-industrial levels, implying that air quality has not yet returned to pre-industrial conditions.
