TI Cave paleozoology in the Judean Desert: assembling records of Holocene
   wild mammal communities
SO JOURNAL OF QUATERNARY SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Anthropocene; carbon stable isotopes; Dead Sea; Levant; radiocarbon
ID EASTERN MEDITERRANEAN REGION; DEAD-SEA LEVELS; SOUTHERN LEVANT; NEGEV
   DESERT; PALEOENVIRONMENTAL RECONSTRUCTION; PALEOCLIMATE RECONSTRUCTION;
   CLIMATE VARIABILITY; SOREQ CAVE; NEAR-EAST; EIN GEDI
AB Long temporal records of Holocene wild mammal communities are essential to examine the role of human impacts and climatic fluctuations in the configuration of modern ecosystems. We show that such records can be assembled through extensive radiocarbon dating of faunal remains obtained from biogenic cave deposits. We dated 110 mammalian remains from 19 different cave sites in the Judean Desert. We use the dates in combination with archaeological survey data and bone collagen/apatite delta C-13 values to study faunal succession in the context of Holocene climate change and human settlement history in the region. Our results suggest a change in the late Holocene, expressed in fewer observations of Arabian leopard (Panthera pardus nimr) and gazelle (Gazella spp.), and an increase of Syrian striped hyena (Hyaena hyaena syriaca), fox (Vulpes spp.), Nubian ibex (Capra nubiana) and rock hyrax (Procavia capensis); suids (Sus scrofa) appear for the first time. According to the data distribution, however, the probability of finding a bone diminishes exponentially with time, which implies that the Judean Desert cave paleozoological record is temporally biased. The weight of evidence ultimately favors an explanation of the observed patterns as the consequence of a combined anthropogenic and climatic impact on local food webs.
