TI Recent environmental change in the southwestern Canadian Plains
SO CANADIAN GEOGRAPHER-GEOGRAPHE CANADIEN
LA English
DT Article; Proceedings Paper
CT Special Session on 20th-Century Environmental Change, at the 1997 Annual
   Meeting of the Canadian-Association-of-Geographers
CY 1997
CL ST JOHNS, CANADA
SP Canadian Assoc Geographers
DE climatic change and variability; southwestern Canadian Plains; proxy
   records; tree rings
ID NORTHERN GREAT-PLAINS; TREE-RING RECORDS; SOUTHERN ALBERTA;
   PROVINCIAL-PARK; WESTERN CANADA; DEVILS LAKE; SAND HILLS; CLIMATE;
   PRAIRIES; 19TH-CENTURY
AB This paper examines 20th-century environmental change in the subhumid southwestern Canadian Plains, specifically in relation to the dominant agricultural landscape and to the climate of the past millennium as reconstructed from proxy data. Anthropogenic landscape change in the last century has been dominated by the conversion of grasslands to ranchland and cropland. This has heightened landscape vulnerability to climatic fluctuations, especially drought. Instrumental climate records, extending back to the 1880s, highlight the variability of precipitation in this region. Proxy environmental records, derived from lake cores and tree-ring analysis, extend this picture into the last millennium and show that drought has been a recurring theme of the Prairie climate. Tree-ring records suggest that some droughts in the last millennium may have exceeded in severity any in the instrumental record. The sustainability of Prairie agriculture depends on adaptation to the amplitudes of climatic change and variability evident in these proxy records.
