TI Geochemical, radiometric, and environmental approaches for the
   assessment of the intensity and chronology of metal contamination in the
   sediment cores from Oualidia lagoon (Morocco)
SO ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE AND POLLUTION RESEARCH
LA English
DT Article
DE Sediment; Metals; Oualidia lagoon; Pb-210 radiometric dating; CRS model;
   CF-CS model; Enrichment factor; The pollution load index
ID ECOLOGICAL RISK-ASSESSMENT; HEAVY-METALS; SURFACE SEDIMENTS; AEGEAN SEA;
   POLLUTION; ATLANTIC; ESTUARY; ZONE; ACCUMULATION; CS-137
AB The present study evaluates the distribution of metals over the last 100 years in the Oualidia lagoon by examining their concentrations in the sediment cores. The samples were analyzed by instrumental neutron activation analysis and inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry. Activities of Pb-210, Ra-226, and Cs-137 were determined by gamma-ray spectrometry for establishing the age-depth relationships throughout the sediment cores by applying conventional models. The results indicated that the study area is contaminated by As and Cd revealing a detectable anthropogenic input of occurring metals as a consequence of the continuous development of human activities around the lagoon since 1950. The enrichment factor calculated for each layer of the three cores revealed that the studied sediments present no enrichment by Pb, K, and Mn; minor enrichment by Zn, Cr, Co, Cu, V, and Ni; and a moderately to severe enrichment by As and Cd. The pollution load index values increase from the bottom to the top of cores, and ranged from 0.9 to 2.8, which indicates levels of pollutants ranging from background to relatively high concentrations in the investigated sediments.
