TI Sediment accumulation and mixing in the Penobscot River and estuary,
   Maine
SO SCIENCE OF THE TOTAL ENVIRONMENT
LA English
DT Article
DE Geochronology; Sedimentation rates; River-estuarine system; Sediment
   transport; Penobscot River
ID PEARL RIVER; RADIONUCLIDE INVENTORIES; SUSPENDED SEDIMENT; MERCURY;
   PB-210; CS-137; RATES; GEOCHRONOLOGY; BAY; TRACERS
AB Mercury (Hg) was discharged in the late 1960s into the Penobscot River by the Holtra-Chemchlor-alkali production facility, which was in operation from 1967 to 2000. To assess the transport and distribution of total Hg, and recovery of the river and estuary system from Hg pollution, physical and radiochemical data were assembled from sediment cores collected from 58 of 72 coring stations sampled in 2009. These stations were located throughout the lower Penobscot River, and included four principal study regions, the Penobscot River (PBR), Mendall Marsh (MM), the Orland River (OR), and the Penobscot estuary (ES). To provide the geochronology required to evaluate sedimentary total Hg profiles, 58 of 72 sediment cores were dated using the atmospheric radionuclide tracers Cs-137, Pb-210, and Pu-239,Pu-240. Sediment cores were assessed for depths of mixing, and for the determination of sediment accumulation rates using both geochemical (total Hg) and radiochemical data. At most stations, evidence for significant vertical mixing, derived from profiles of Be-7 (where possible) and porosity, was restricted to the upper similar to 1-3 cm. Thus, historic profiles of both total Hg and radionuclides were only minimally distorted, allowing a reconstruction of their depositional history. The pulse input tracers Cs-137 and Pu-239,Pu-240 used to assess sediment accumulation rates agreed well, while the steady state tracer Pb-210 exhibited weaker agreement, likely due to irregular lateral sediment inputs. (C) 2018 Published by Elsevier B.V.
